{"id":"g-apple-card-what","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"text","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":true,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":"Restates that Chase will become the new Apple Card issuer and that it entered a forward purchase commitment on Dec 30, 2025 to acquire the Apple credit card portfolio with closing in about 24 months, adding consistent exposure figures.","answer":"On January 7, 2026, JPMorganChase announced that **Chase will become the new issuer of the Apple Card**. The Firm had entered into a forward purchase commitment on December 30, 2025 to acquire the Apple credit card portfolio from the prior issuer, with an expected closing in approximately 24 months (the \u0022Apple Card transaction\u0022). At closing, the Firm\u0027s estimated total credit exposure is approximately **$104 billion**, including roughly **$23 billion** of estimated drawn loans [1][4]. As of June 30, 2026, this exposure had grown to approximately **$106 billion** [7].\n\n**Sources:** [1][4][7]","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":25231,"outputTokens":302,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":10370,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":true,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["de4a4a76-f1f5-4a12-8e19-c5f208313058","4594ae18-35e0-4227-87a1-fed45756ded3","11e0eedd-8ed0-4061-b543-a2c33516cd3d","262bd853-90c9-4d46-b4fc-d4b548bb8603","5ea16272-ffa7-416e-af65-e0531d473005","d386dc2d-89f4-439b-8097-60272c69ecd0","f43c291a-46e7-4d3a-ad1e-013cac8e5b1f","e8f132f8-eb41-42ce-8f27-5ac9656ad856","ef4d1724-30d1-4101-a0df-a444456f139d","a97d4268-4cee-4d48-a7fa-7bd96796be06"],"turns":2,"agentCostUsd":0.0480832}
{"id":"g-apple-card-allowance","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"number","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":true,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":null,"answer":"The Apple Card transaction added **$2.2 billion** to JPMorgan Chase\u0027s allowance for credit losses in Q4 2025 [2][6][10].","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":25820,"outputTokens":150,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":7634,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":true,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["945b78aa-f4a8-419b-a0fa-c7c6560f740f","f8adbcb7-27fb-40f3-933f-b7918102e2d9","ce120d6a-0a4b-4c07-80a3-eafa892f8e44","4594ae18-35e0-4227-87a1-fed45756ded3","641336df-3081-4bd0-9e01-456711e99a1b","ffceffe8-fe8f-4965-856c-90cb06867154","e8f132f8-eb41-42ce-8f27-5ac9656ad856","005e9cc3-6b82-4666-acad-c4f76b6750c4","31c946da-4351-4d1a-b1f0-9a291f0ecb55","f49fff94-23c2-4f57-aca2-223f2f8101b7"],"turns":2,"agentCostUsd":0.048913200000000004}
{"id":"g-expense-why","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"text","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":true,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":"Matches reference \u2014 cites higher compensation (revenue-related pay and more employees) plus higher brokerage/distribution fees, auto lease depreciation, tech/marketing investment and occupancy expense.","answer":"JPMorgan Chase\u0027s noninterest expense rose to $95.6 billion in 2025, up 4% from 2024 [9]. Key drivers [1][9]:\n\n- **Higher compensation expense** \u2014 growth in employee headcount (primarily front office roles) and higher revenue-related compensation, predominantly in CIB and AWM [1]\n- **Higher noncompensation expense**, including:\n  - Higher brokerage expense (CIB) and distribution fees (AWM)\n  - Higher depreciation on auto operating lease assets (CCB)\n  - Continued investments in technology across lines of business and Corporate, and marketing (CCB)\n  - Higher occupancy expense from property additions/improvements, including the new headquarters, bank branches, and other corporate offices\n\nFor context, total compensation expense grew to $54.5 billion (from $51.4 billion) and total noncompensation expense rose to $41.2 billion (from $40.4 billion) [2].","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":24791,"outputTokens":417,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":10546,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":true,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["7a42b421-6450-43e5-956f-a7b8b28bc4cc","02bf08f3-cae5-472a-80d6-f3f9dc4cfae4","7a25e8aa-9c7a-4447-a215-3ba75035fe40","b2a873df-793d-4c60-ae30-a9a925796555","b5b538c4-2bb9-4c2f-bba2-1e50cc10cbb0","b2e6df19-d3cc-4649-a079-1ce7e5e19c24","c65ef18d-f436-4cda-81a7-ac5025869a9a","887eeaa7-a03c-4b62-a88c-1b5061c6736b","9c9192d3-6668-48f3-8c61-918db7d000e3","1968fe39-f473-4435-8c29-35b531510807"],"turns":2,"agentCostUsd":0.0474932}
{"id":"g-nir-why-q2-26","category":"prose","tenant":"globex","expectedKind":"text","repeat":0,"labelable":true,"firstHitRank":null,"retrievedTokens":0,"correct":false,"refused":false,"truncated":null,"judgeReason":"The answer omits the reference\u0027s note about higher auto operating lease income and slightly misstates the Visa-related gain (says $4.5B vs $4.6B).","answer":"## Why JPMorgan\u0027s Q2 2026 noninterest revenue jumped\n\nFirmwide noninterest revenue rose 47% to $31.8B (from $21.7B in Q2 2025) [3]. The surge was driven primarily by a large one-time gain in \u0022other income,\u0022 which itself was up to $7.5B from $1.2B:\n\n- **$4.5 billion initial gain on the Visa share exchange**, recognized on May 11, 2026 [1][6]\n- **$1.0 billion of gains on certain equity investments** \u2014 a measurement-alternative markup plus gains from transitioning certain equity investments to recurring fair value ($763M in Corporate, $263M in CIB) [1][6]\n\nBeyond that one-time item, the rest of the increase reflected broad-based growth across core fee lines [6]:\n- Investment banking fees up 28% ($3.2B), led by equity underwriting fees (\u002B78%) tied to large IPOs/convertible offerings, and higher advisory and debt underwriting fees [per CIB commentary]\n- Principal transactions up 26% ($9.0B), driven by higher Equity Markets revenue (Equity Derivatives, Prime Finance, Cash), partly offset by lower Fixed Income Markets\n- Asset management fees up 18% ($5.7B) on higher average market levels and net inflows\n- Commissions and other fees up 19%, and lending/deposit-related fees up 12%\n\nSo the jump was a combination of a ~$5.5B one-time boost (Visa share exchange gain \u002B equity investment gains) layered on top of genuine growth in investment banking, trading, and asset management fees.","citedSourceIds":[],"inputTokens":46691,"outputTokens":1026,"reasoningTokens":0,"latencyMs":19353,"serverTotalMs":null,"sources":[],"retrievedRelevant":false,"toolCalls":["mcp__lumen__search_filings","mcp__lumen__search_filings","mcp__lumen__search_filings"],"seenChunkIds":["d66a0957-77b6-44a0-94d0-26e1cdf0999f","f12dfc84-2c6d-447b-af15-9f0b934b27ab","7511f283-8e91-4d69-9e72-3e37964dce84","b01f580d-fb81-4be2-987f-3a7f19ec5178","4ba5f646-643d-490c-89ff-e1f5c4f9c821","688f3390-58ef-4bc1-8767-ebb3e20e5f04","596a1122-aeb1-453d-bd4f-6f7cac94500e","e427b93c-e06c-4f25-9e92-0c55df6778d5","73ade550-612e-475d-bd30-030842a52244","804733f1-e47f-46cc-94e4-dca3fcd4fc52","dcc772c8-a971-4b48-8e58-1d53bcc96ed1","dcea2a4e-e978-4dda-b966-50418225b0ee","7ca996c8-bf6f-436a-932a-c271d78ae2e5","729b21f0-ecf9-4331-9a34-7f3c3ba75679","be63f595-cfb9-4061-80cc-c16c2636f249","8a57b7dd-721b-4798-bfee-bf749f17409f","a47c8028-1113-4302-a984-8791b177afb3","ec381347-263c-4a0b-87d8-45351a83b518","fb017807-de28-4b01-8db5-796df7dc9c16","16419763-7fc3-490c-a831-e277b44d9929","4474d82a-53d3-4080-9c8a-f41c70f1e03c","4d805414-c345-427f-8583-9e7797969e56","6defaa9f-db87-4a52-8fbe-67cecf49a7a6","0b043980-abb8-4210-b504-3403a2294ebf","8d57c3ce-d137-4b1b-8009-8985e2bdb3e8","85d7d0a1-2d4d-4887-a32f-596ca01b4e78","ba2e2154-4d95-4489-af49-a26f74529d66","64eab717-4b08-4fd2-a7bc-5312030d5032","55d04e77-f377-48eb-93b2-6a24b7295e53"],"turns":4,"agentCostUsd":0.08815519999999999}
